jank-12 + jank-21: small-integer arithmetic in i64, i64 integer literals, + - * throw on overflow
(cohost doc/upstream/jank-12-i32-add.md, doc/upstream/jank-21-int32-loop-local.md; lane U1, order U1-1)

Base: jank-lang/jank f3d82d65499920f81216e9235bd5d3a34a968e3f. Apply from the clone's root: git apply <this file>.

Files:
- compiler+runtime/include/cpp/jank/runtime/core/math.hpp: every integer x integer arm of add, sub,
  mul computes in i64 (a small integer's i32 payload and a 32-bit native operand no longer wrap at
  32 bits) and throws "integer overflow" on i64 overflow, as inc throws "Overflow on increment.";
  reals, ratios and big numbers keep the plain operator. +' (promoting_add etc.) is unchanged.
  - unboxed arms: detail::checked_{add,sub,mul}: __builtin_*_overflow into i64; no check when both
    operands are 32 bit (it cannot overflow).
  - boxed arms: detail::boxed_{add,sub,mul}: when both operands fit in i32, the op runs in i32 and,
    without overflow, the result is tagged directly (one `jo` more than the old wrapping arm);
    otherwise detail::boxed_*_slow (out of line, cold) does the checked i64 op and boxes it.
  - the helpers take operands by const & and are always_inline but not flatten: by value, or
    flattened, every big_integer/ratio/big_decimal arm of the visit inlined copies and allocations
    (add(object_ref, object_ref) grew from 1090 to 5295 instructions at -O3; now 1166) and
    fib-30-plain ran 22% slower; that measurement is why they are shaped this way.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/runtime/core/math.cpp: detail::throw_integer_overflow and the
  three boxed_*_slow functions.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/analyze/cpp_util.cpp: an unboxed integer literal is typed `long`
  (`long long` on Windows), never `int`, so a let/loop local initialized from a literal is 64 bit
  (mutable_type keeps the literal's type; recur converts into it). literal_codegen_type only maps an
  integer to a ref type when boxed.
- compiler+runtime/src/cpp/jank/codegen/cpp_processor.cpp: the unboxed literal is spelled with that
  type (`2147483647l`; i64 min as `(-9223372036854775807l - 1l)`), in both the eval (JIT) and the
  module (AOT) constant paths; before, `auto v(0);` made the local an int whatever the analyzer said.

Repro (cohost): bash dev/research/upstream/run.sh 12-i32-add; bash dev/research/upstream/run.sh 21-int32-loop-local
Expected: the drafts' "Expected" lines, plus the cases appended under U1-1 in each repro.
Known side effect: an interop call whose C++ overloads differ only between int and long long, called
with a bare literal, is now ambiguous where it picked the int overload; cast the literal.

